{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>When you bring, etc., on your Shavuot.</b> It intends to say that the festival is not merely for the sign of the new meal-offering alone, but also for the completion of the counting of the weeks, for it is a great tikkun for the souls of the people of Israel, as stated in the books of the men of truth. And it was precise to say ״בְּשָׁבֻעֹתֵיכֶם״ with the plural suffix, to hint that the seven weeks are the aspect of the seven righteous ones, the foundations of the people of Israel, as stated in the Zohar (Tikkunim 21a)." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>בְּשָׁבֻעֹתֵיכֶם</b> to exclude the opinion of fools (Menahot 65b) who hold that when the verse says ״מִמָּחֳרַת הַשַּׁבָּת״ (Leviticus 23:15) it means the Sabbath of Creation. Therefore it says <b>בְּשָׁבֻעֹתֵיכֶם</b> — meaning the weeks that are yours; and if the meaning of the statement ״מִמָּחֳרַת הַשַּׁבָּת״ is the Sabbath of Creation, then these seventh days of theirs would be nothing but the seventh days of all the world. Something similar we find that they said in a baraita (Menahot 72a), and this is their language: Rabbi Yehudah says, ״תִּסְפָּר לָךְ״ (Deuteronomy 16:9) — the Sabbath of Creation has gone out, whose counting is incumbent upon every person."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:49.919285123Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}